/**

\page tarch_tests Unit tests

`src/unittests` contains the unit tests in a directory tree that follows `src`: The tests of
`src/tarch/la/Scalar.h` are in `src/unittests/tarch/la/ScalarTest.cpp`. A test file is one `.cpp` file
without a header. With `-DBUILD_TESTING=ON`, CMake builds the tests into one executable per build mode
and dimension, such as `UnitTestsRelease2D`.

Writing a unit test
-------------------

A test file includes the code under test and `tarch/testing/UnitTest.h`. It states every property it
checks as one unit test:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
UNIT_TEST("The power of a scalar raises its base to the exponent") {
  GIVEN("The base four and the exponent one half") {
    constexpr double Base     = 4.0;
    constexpr double Exponent = 0.5;

    WHEN("The power is taken") {
      const double power = tarch::la::pow(Base, Exponent);

      THEN("The power is two") {
        VALIDATE_NUMERICAL_EQUALS(power, 2.0);
      }
    }
  }
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A `GIVEN` block defines the inputs. A `WHEN` block performs the action and stores its results. A `THEN`
block checks them. `AND_WHEN` and `AND_THEN` continue a sequence of actions and checks. Every
description is a sentence with a capital first letter. The report prints it after "Given:", "When:" and
"Then:". A test uses the public interface of the code under test only.

The body of a unit test runs once for every innermost block. A run enters at most one block per nesting
level. Every innermost block starts from the state that its enclosing blocks set up. Sibling blocks never
read each other's changes.

Four macros check results:

| Macro | Fails when |
|---|---|
| `VALIDATE(condition, ...)` | the condition is false |
| `VALIDATE_EQUALS(actual, expected, ...)` | `actual == expected` is false. Two integers compare by value whatever their signedness. |
| `VALIDATE_NUMERICAL_EQUALS(actual, expected, ...)` | the values differ by more than tarch::testing::NumericalTolerance |
| `VALIDATE_NUMERICAL_BUFFERS_EQUALS(actual, expected, ...)` | the buffers differ in size or an entry differs by more than tarch::testing::MaxUnitsInTheLastPlace units in the last place of the largest magnitude of expected |

A failed check records the open blocks, the file, the line, the statement and the values of its
operands. It prints every further argument with its value and returns from the enclosing function. In
the body of a unit test this return ends the run. In a helper function the caller continues after the
return. The macros evaluate every operand once. Checks work in
helper functions, lambdas, tasks and parallel loops. The runner catches an exception of a unit test and
reports it as a failure of that unit test.

The executable runs every test inside one OpenMP parallel region. A test spreads its work inside this
region with tarch::parallelFor or with tasks. Every rank runs every unit
test. After every run of a unit test the ranks agree on the blocks of the next run. A test never puts a
block inside a branch that depends on the rank. A check that leaves a run on one rank before a
collective blocks the other ranks in that collective. A test calls collectives in its `GIVEN` and `WHEN`
blocks and checks the results in the `THEN` blocks after them.

Running the tests
-----------------

`make test` or `ctest` runs one entry per build mode, dimension, library and rank layout, for example
`UnitTestsRelease2D-peano4-3Ranks`. The executable takes three options:

| Option | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--filter pattern` | runs the unit tests whose name matches the pattern. A `*` matches any text and a `?` one character. The name of a unit test is the path of its file below `src/unittests` without the extension, a slash and its description. Several filters add up. |
| `--list` | lists the names of the selected unit tests without running them |
| `--junit file` | writes a JUnit report. GitLab shows the report in a merge request. |

Rank 0 prints one line per test file with its number of unit tests, checks and run time. It prints the
failures of all ranks with the reporting ranks and the open blocks. It names the unit tests without a
check in a file with checks and closes with a summary.

*/
